    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the build is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nI consolidated the three separate duplicate checks (telephone, email, household) into a single derived **identity key**.\n\n**New, focused units** (in `rest/controller`):\n- **`OwnerIdentity`** \u2014 the one place that derives the key: `normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + (email or empty) + \"|\" + (householdId or empty)`. Reused by both the guard and the response mapper, so the definition lives in exactly one spot.\n- **`IdentityDuplicateValidator`** \u2014 the single guard: rejects a candidate only when its *whole* key equals an existing owner's key.\n- **`DuplicateIdentityException`** \u2014 the 409 signal.\n\n**Controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`)** \u2014 replaced the three inline checks (telephone lookup, `HouseholdDuplicateValidator`, `EmailUniquenessValidator`) with one `identityDuplicateValidator.validate(owner)` call, run after telephone/email are normalized and after any shared-household assignment, so all three key segments are canonical before comparison. `HouseholdAssigner` is retained \u2014 sharing a household is a feature, not a duplicate check \u2014 but the standalone household *rejection* is gone.\n\n**Advice** \u2014 collapsed the three 409 handlers into one `DuplicateIdentityException` handler.\n\n**API** \u2014 added a read-only `identityKey` to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` and mapped it in `OwnerMapper` via `OwnerIdentity`, so every owner response now returns its key.\n\n**Removed** the now-dead `DuplicateTelephoneException`, `DuplicateEmailException`, `DuplicateHouseholdException`, `EmailUniquenessValidator`, and `HouseholdDuplicateValidator`.\n\n## Behaviour change (as specified)\nTwo owners in the same household (same `householdId`) with different telephones now have different identity keys and are **both allowed** \u2014 only an exact full-key match is a 409. The acceptance test (`same telephone \u2192 409`) passes because two owners with matching telephone, both with empty email and empty household, produce identical keys.",
